I’ll first inspect the pattern metadata and the frozen PXRD workflow, then build a chemically plausible phase inventory before fitting. I’ll keep the refinement residual-led and will verify the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and final GSAS-II project against one another.

The chemistry points first to Mn–W–O phases after a 900 °C MnO + WO₃ firing, but residual MnO/WO₃ and manganese tungstate polymorphs remain legitimate candidates. I’m now locating the supplied solver helpers and screening the observed peak families before committing any structure to refinement.

Peak positions and relative intensities strongly support wolframite-type MnWO₄ (P2/c): every major family from 15.7° through ~98° has a counterpart, while Mn₃WO₆, MnO, Mn oxides, and WO₃ polymorphs predict strong unobserved lines. I’m treating those as a watch list rather than discarding them, and moving to a GSAS-II baseline so that weak residual families can be judged under a common profile.

The initial Rietveld model is chemically coherent and already accounts for the full peak inventory, but its residuals are intensity- and line-shape-dominated (Rwp ≈ 7.8%), not random noise. Before accepting coordinate shifts, I’m testing polarization, symmetry-distinct March–Dollase axes, roughness, and size/strain allocations; the first unconstrained oxygen-coordinate trial moved farther than the generator’s plausible reference mismatch and is therefore being treated as diagnostic rather than accepted.

A coherent minor phase has emerged: monoclinic WO₃ gives ~2.0 wt% and lowers Rwp from 7.425% to 7.097%, with support from its partly unique 26–35° families. The triclinic and orthorhombic WO₃ alternatives fit less well; MnO/Mn oxides are at trace or unstable scales. I’m rebuilding the common two-phase profile and will re-challenge those watch-list phases against it before finalizing the inventory.

The inventory now strongly favors wolframite MnWO₄ with about 2 wt% monoclinic WO₃. I’m rebuilding the shared two-phase profile and then re-testing the remaining oxide watch list so that the minor phase and fraction are judged under the final nuisance model, not the earlier single-phase fit.

The rebuilt two-phase baseline is stable at Rwp 7.097%, with 97.994 wt% MnWO₄ and 2.006 wt% monoclinic WO₃. Its profile, displacement, cells, scales, background, polarization, and the supported MnWO₄ [110] texture all reconverged without parameter-bound failures; I’m now challenging MnO and manganese-oxide alternatives against this exact baseline.

The watch-list signal is not behaving like a real third phase. The apparent 0.56 wt% MnO gain comes almost entirely from one peak overlapping a strong MnWO₄ reflection near 41.2°; adding MnO actually worsens its expected isolated families near 35.5° and 59.5°. Mn₂O₃ is only 0.23 wt% and similarly lacks a coherent multi-family improvement, while Mn₃O₄ and Mn₃WO₆ collapse to zero scale. I’m therefore retaining these as rejected/upper-limit candidates and checking model-record sensitivity before finalizing the two-phase structure.

The unrestrained oxygen-coordinate fit is statistically strong and chemically plausible, but it moves O by about 0.10 Å from the only exact MnWO₄ library record—larger than the normal reference-mismatch range. That record was measured at 2.4 GPa, so I’m checking ambient wolframite analogues before deciding whether those shifts are genuine ambient structure information or an intensity surrogate.

The alternative-record audit is consistent: a second rock-salt MnO record drops to only 0.21 wt%, wurtzite MnO and both Mn₃O₄ records collapse to zero, and both Mn₂O₃ symmetry records remain around 0.2 wt% with negligible, non-coherent gains. The two-phase inventory is robust; the remaining comparison is between two ambient monoclinic WO₃ structural records.

A second nuisance-model check changed the structural interpretation materially: once the two-phase coordinates are in place, a physical low-angle surface-roughness term improves Rwp from 6.763% to 6.432%, with \(A=0.217\) and \(B=0.138\), both inside the stated synthesis/measurement range. Because those two coefficients are 99.5% correlated, I’m reducing this to a one-parameter roughness model before re-refining coordinates; this should prevent oxygen positions from compensating for low-angle attenuation.

The physically constrained model is now coherent: a one-parameter roughness representation, fixed \(B=0.14\), gives refined \(A=0.070\); polarization is held at its stated lower physical bound (0.50), MnWO₄ \(U_\mathrm{iso}=0.00600\) Å², and all coordinate shifts return to the expected small-reference range. It converges at Rwp 6.193% with 97.77/2.23 wt%. I’m doing the required final watch-list replay under this exact restrained model before exporting.

The final-profile replay reveals a real residual-led inventory change: triclinic WO₃ refines to about 0.94 wt% in addition to monoclinic WO₃ and improves several separated families (≈23.6, 29.1, 33.6, 41.7, 55.7°), not just one overlap. I’m releasing its cell only now—after scale acceptance—and rebuilding the three-phase profile. A ~0.3 wt% Mn₂O₃ signal remains on the watch list until this rebuild shows whether it survives.

The phase inventory has converged to wolframite MnWO₄ plus two weak WO₃ polymorphs. I’m now packaging the exact selected refinement, exporting the CIFs/profile, and replay-checking that every submitted number agrees with the final GSAS-II project.

The selected nuisance model is physically restrained: one specimen shift, an 8-term smooth background, common nonnegative U/W/X widths, one reduced roughness degree of freedom, and only a weak [001] texture term on MnWO₄. Unsupported size/strain/asymmetry terms and trace oxide candidates have been pruned; the triclinic WO₃ cell remains fixed because its weak scale does not support stable cell refinement.

The independent read-only replay has passed: the project is unchanged, all three phases regenerate atomically, the submitted CIFs byte-match the replay exports, and GSAS-II reports no negative-width, negative-background, or polarization validity flags. The replayed profile gives Rwp = 6.15973% and GOF = 2.23719.

One important caveat surfaced in the physical audit: the best-matching 1960 monoclinic WO₃ record has low-precision oxygen coordinates that generate 1.58 Å O···O contacts. I’m doing the required same-profile challenge against the later neutron-diffraction WO₃ model before deciding whether the profile evidence justifies retaining that structurally uncertain record.

The physical-source challenge resolves the caveat cleanly: replacing only the low-precision 1960 oxygen sites with 1966 neutron oxygen coordinates removes all short-contact flags while retaining the X-ray-sensitive W sublattice. It also gives a slightly lower Rwp than the unmodified record, so this physically screened hybrid is now the selected monoclinic WO₃ model.

Completed. The modeled crystalline inventory is:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| Wolframite MnWO₄ | 97.433 ± 0.071 wt% |
| Monoclinic WO₃ | 1.660 ± 0.096 wt% |
| Triclinic WO₃ | 0.907 ± 0.106 wt% |

Atomic replay gives Rwp 6.15942% and GOF 2.23708. All CIFs pass the geometry/occupancy screen. The WO₃ polymorph split and minor-phase atomic coordinates remain less reliable than their combined 2.567 wt% abundance.

